{
  "image_name": "1e791f6dce8e6f0a409e57833b3a5599.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how ALDH2 deficiency may drive vascular disease through two related pathways. On the left, deficient ALDH2 increases the aldehyde 4‑HNE, which activates signaling molecules including NF‑κB, HIF1α, ERK1/2, and Beclin. These pathways promote mitochondrial damage responses, especially DRP1 phosphorylation–linked mitochondrial fission and autophagosome formation, along with induction of MMP2 and MMP9. The overall effect is increased proliferation of pulmonary artery smooth muscle cells (PASMCs), shown as thickening of the vessel wall and narrowing of the lumen, leading to pulmonary arterial hypertension.\n\nOn the right, ALDH2 deficiency appears to alter smooth muscle cell gene regulation in the aorta. A dashed connection points to Max, which suppresses pre‑microRNA‑31, while blocked processing of the pre‑microRNA‑31 duplex is also indicated. This relieves inhibition of MYOCD, allowing MYOCD together with SRF to increase expression of contractile genes such as calponin, SM22α, and αSMA. The diagram links this transcriptional program to smooth muscle phenotypic switching between synthetic and contractile states and to aortic aneurysm or dissection. Overall, the image connects mitochondrial/aldehyde stress with smooth muscle remodeling in pulmonary and aortic disease.",
